<DocPage
	eyebrow="Hardware"
	title="What it runs on"
	description="HippOS runs on x86-64 PCs and handhelds, and on the Raspberry Pi 4 and 5. This is what the current beta supports and what's still rough."
>
	<h2 id="minimum-specs">Minimum specs</h2>
	<p>Enough to boot HippOS and play older systems on a PC. The lowest hardware a beta tester has run it on is a 2016 Celeron N3160 with 4 GB of RAM. For the Raspberry Pi, see <a href="#raspberry-pi">Raspberry Pi</a>.</p>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>CPU:</strong> 64-bit x86 (amd64).</li>
		<li><strong>RAM:</strong> 4 GB.</li>
		<li><strong>Firmware:</strong> UEFI boot.</li>
		<li><strong>Storage:</strong> 32 GB or more to boot from USB, and at least 16 GB for an internal install. Use more if you want your games on the same drive.</li>
		<li><strong>Graphics:</strong> any GPU supported by Linux. See <a href="#graphics">Graphics</a>.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Recommended</h2>
	<p>
		For PlayStation 2, GameCube, Wii and newer, emulation needs much more power. Testers run these on a
		6-core CPU from roughly the last five years (for example a Ryzen 5 4500U or Core i7-10700), 8 GB of RAM or
		more, and a GPU with Vulkan support. PlayStation 3, Switch and Xbox 360 emulators want more again.
	</p>

	<h2 id="graphics">Graphics</h2>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>AMD and Intel:</strong> the main targets, using Mesa's open OpenGL and Vulkan drivers. Most modern emulators expect Vulkan.</li>
		<li><strong>NVIDIA GTX 16 / RTX 20 and newer:</strong> supported with NVIDIA's open kernel driver.</li>
		<li><strong>Older NVIDIA cards:</strong> HippOS includes the legacy 580, 470, 390 and 340 driver branches and picks the right one at boot. If none fits, it falls back to nouveau.</li>
		<li><strong>Hybrid laptops (AMD/Intel + NVIDIA):</strong> supported, with GPU set-up handled at boot.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Handhelds</h2>
	<p>
		AMD handhelds such as the Steam Deck, ROG Ally, Legion Go and AYANEO devices get TDP control through
		<code>ryzenadj</code>, along with brightness and volume hotkeys. Handheld support is the area that changes most
		between betas. Check the Discord for your exact model.
	</p>

	<h2 id="raspberry-pi">Raspberry Pi</h2>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Models:</strong> Raspberry Pi 4 and Raspberry Pi 5, from one image (<code>hippos-arm64.img.zst</code>). Most testing so far has been on a Pi 5 with 8 GB of RAM.</li>
		<li><strong>Storage:</strong> a microSD card or USB drive of 32 GB or more. A fast card (A2 rated) or a USB SSD makes loading noticeably quicker.</li>
		<li><strong>What it plays:</strong> classic consoles and handhelds and PlayStation run well on a Pi 5, and lighter PlayStation 2 and Wii U games work too. Newer systems are hit and miss, so check the Discord for reports. Xbox, Xbox 360 and PlayStation 3 aren't available: the Pi can't run them.</li>
		<li><strong>Windows games</strong> run through Wine with box64. Expect older and lighter games only.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Controllers &amp; input</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Xbox, PlayStation, Switch Pro and most generic USB/Bluetooth pads work out of the box. Xbox wireless pads get the <code>xpadneo</code> and <code>xone</code> drivers.</li>
		<li>Arcade encoders (IPAC, X-Arcade, JammaSD) are remapped to virtual gamepads by Pad2Key.</li>
		<li>Light guns and wheels are supported in emulators that offer them.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Displays</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>HippOS starts at 1080p60 by default and follows display hotplug in both EmulationStation and desktop mode.</li>
		<li>CRT output is supported through Switchres, with modelines generated automatically. Turn it on explicitly in settings.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Networking</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Wi-Fi and Bluetooth use upstream Linux firmware.</li>
		<li>Realtek USB Wi-Fi dongles that appear as a CD-ROM drive are switched to network mode automatically.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Help us test</h2>
	<p>
		Hardware coverage grows from beta reports. If something on your machine doesn't work, upload your logs (see
		<a href="/faq">the FAQ</a>) and post in the Discord, including your device model.
	</p>
</DocPage>
